## SECTION 2 — LESSON IDENTIFICATION TABLE
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Community News</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Our Community Is Famous For...</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: reading_1: Identify specific information from fictional short texts.<br>reading_2: Identify the sequence of events in simple stories.<br>phonemic_awareness: Demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by identifying short and long vowel sounds in orally stated single-syllable words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can identify specific information about community events in short fictional texts (e.g., 'The school organized a fair last week.' 'The community held a clean-up day at the park.').<br>Can recognize the sequence of events in simple stories about community traditions (e.g., Students read a story about the Desfile de las Mil Polleras and identify the order of events: 'First, the participants gathered, then the parade started, and finally, they celebrated with music and food.').<br>Can identify short and long vowel sounds in words related to community events (e.g., recognizing /æ/ in 'banner' vs. /eɪ/ in 'parade' or /ɪ/ in 'tradition' vs. /iː/ in 'queen' when reading about local celebrations).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

## SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE
<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays flashcards or pictures of community events and traditions (e.g., `festival`, `parade`, `mural`, `community fair`, `Pollera`, `sombrero Pintao`) reviewed in Lesson 1. The teacher asks students, "What did we talk about in the last class?" and "What did you see in the pictures?" using simple past tense. Students recall and say words like "Carnival" or "Pollera" or "They celebrated."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a picture of a news reporter and says, "This is a `reporter` for `Our Community News`. Today, we will read a news story about a `cultural event` in our community." The teacher models reading a short sentence from a sample news story, "Our community `celebrated` a `colorful festival` last weekend." The teacher points to `celebrated` and highlights the past tense, saying /d/ sound. The teacher also models identifying short /æ/ in 'banner' and long /eɪ/ in 'parade' by emphasizing the sounds orally.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does 'celebrated' mean something is happening now or did it happen before?" (Expected answer: before/past)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a `parade` a big event with many people walking, or a small quiet meeting?" (Expected answer: big event/many people)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Community News".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher distributes a short, fictional "Community News Report" titled "San Miguel `Celebrated` a Great `Community Fair`!" The text is presented as a newspaper clipping with a picture of a `community fair` or `festival`. It describes the fair using `past simple tense` verbs (e.g., `organized`, `visited`, `prepared`, `gathered`, `liked`, `didn't like`), compound sentences with `but`, and target vocabulary (`reporter`, `mural`, `artisan`, `traditional`, `colorful`, `Pollera`, `sombrero Pintao`). The report is approximately 50-70 words long.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Identify specific information from fictional short texts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students read the news report silently. The teacher asks, "What is this news report mainly about?" and "Which famous `cultural event` did San Miguel celebrate?" (e.g., a community fair, the Desfile de las Mil Polleras, a Carnival).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Activity:</b> The teacher writes 3-4 simple True/False statements on the board related to the general content of the text. E.g., "The fair happened last week." "Many `artisans` sold `traditional` products." Students read the text again to identify if the statements are true or false.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Identifying specific information and sequence of events in short texts, practicing `past simple tense` and `compound sentences with 'but'`, and identifying `short and long vowel sounds`.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Information Hunt:</b> Students re-read the news report. The teacher provides a worksheet with `who, what, where, when, how` questions. E.g., "<b>When</b> did the `community fair` happen?" "<b>Who</b> `participated` in preparing the `mural`?" "<b>What</b> `traditional` items did the `artisans` sell?" Students write short answers based on the text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Sequencing Events:</b> The teacher distributes strips of paper with 3-4 key events from the news report, jumbled. E.g., "The `artisans` `gathered`." "Students `prepared` a `mural`." "The fair `opened` with music." Students work individually or in pairs to put the events in the correct `sequence of events` as described in the text, using linking words like 'First', 'Then', 'Finally'. The teacher walks around, checking for correct order and `past simple` verb usage.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Vowel Sounds & Preferences:</b> Students work in pairs. They identify words from the text that have `short and long vowel sounds` (e.g., `fair` /eɪ/, `last` /æ/, `event` /ɛ/, `queen` /iː/, `visited` /ɪ/, `tradition` /ɪ/, `parade` /eɪ/, `banner` /æ/). They say these words aloud to each other. Then, they take turns reading sentences from the text that express preferences or past experiences (e.g., "I `liked` the `colorful floats` during the `Carnival parade`, `but` I `didn't like` the loud music."). They discuss with their partner if they agree or disagree, practicing softening disagreement (e.g., "I like parades, too, but I prefer...").</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Community News".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Our Community News Report - Read and Share"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a short, new fictional news snippet (2-3 sentences) about a different `cultural event` or community activity from "Our Community News." This snippet will use `past simple tense` and some of the descriptive `adjectives` (e.g., 'A `friendly` `reporter` `interviewed` the `tall` `Carnival Queen` about her `colorful` dress.').</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each student reads their snippet carefully.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Then, one student reads their news snippet aloud to their partner. The partner listens and identifies: 1) one specific piece of information (e.g., `who` or `what` happened), and 2) one `adjective` used to `describe` a person or event.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They switch roles. The second student reads their snippet, and the first student performs the identification task.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Finally, the pairs briefly share with the class one interesting fact they `read` about or `described` (e.g., "Our news was about a `chubby` `artisan` who made `strong` baskets.").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in reading, collaborative interaction, and successfully extracting specific information and descriptive language from the text.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Observation Checklist (teacher observes pairs during the "Read and Share" activity) and a short individual Reading Quiz.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students complete a short individual reading quiz based on a new, very brief (2-3 sentence) news item about a community event (e.g., "Last week, the `neighborhood` `organized` a `clean-up day` at the park. Many `friendly` people `participated` `before` the sun was `strong`. `Finally`, they `celebrated` with food.").</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. Answer one `wh-` question (e.g., "What happened last week?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. Put two sentences in correct `sequence of events` order from the text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. Identify one `past simple` verb and one `adjective`.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correctly identifies specific information (e.g., who, what, when).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correctly recognizes the `sequence of events` in simple stories.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates understanding of target `vocabulary` and `past simple tense` in context.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Oral observation during Production): Intelligible pronunciation of key words, including demonstrating some awareness of short/long vowel sounds.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following prompts on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can identify (most/some/few) important details in the community news stories."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"It was (easy/ok/difficult) to understand the order of events."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new thing I learned about reading community news today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of reading today's news did I like best? Why?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">What worked well in this reading lesson? Students showed good engagement with the "Community News Report" format and were able to identify specific details and sequence events reasonably well, especially after guided practice. The `phonemic awareness` activity was a good touch point for `short and long vowel sounds` with words from the text.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? Some students struggled with the `compound sentences` using `but`; more explicit modeling or examples might be beneficial. For Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction), I will encourage students to use the `past simple tense` and `adjectives for describing people` to talk about the events and community members they read about today. They will also practice sharing past experiences and preferences, building on the content from today's texts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson, students will practice speaking and interacting, using the vocabulary and grammar from today's reading to discuss community events and describe people, continuing with the "Our Community Is Famous For..." theme.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>